EBITDA does not cover interest. FCF is -$12.4B on $2.2B of revenue — a ratio that implies the business is in heavy build-out mode. Several bonds carry very low coupons (0.5%, 1%, 1.25%), suggesting convertible or structured issuance rather than straight high-yield. No near-term maturities, but the funding dependency is structural.

Framework scorecard: Same pattern as CoreWeave. Smaller scale, earlier-stage, higher relative burn. Borrower-seat equity is an option on the AI cycle continuing and refinancing markets remaining open.

Cyclical Sellers — Micron, Western Digital, Seagate
Current position: Peak-cycle metrics. The question is where normalised margins settle.

Company               Latest Q Revenue Gross Margin FCF (latest Q) Net Cash/(Debt) Inventory
Micron (MU)           $54.2B           87.0%        $32.9B         +$68.3B         $10.4B   
Western Digital (WDC) $4.4B            58.1%        $1.4B          +$0.4B          $1.8B    
Seagate (STX)         $4.2B            57.8%        $1.5B          -$1.4B          $1.7B    

Micron's revenue quadrupled in four quarters (from $13.6B to $54.2B), and gross margin expanded from 56.8% to 87.0%. These are peak-cycle numbers by any historical standard. Memory supply constraints are expected to persist through 2030, with 2027 DRAM growth forecast at mid-20%. (12)

The AI-led memory cycle is viewed as longer and stronger than prior cycles, with the industry described as sold out for 2027, and possible softening expected around 2028. (13)

However, current AI infrastructure supply may be exceeding underlying end-user demand, and a correction toward alignment with a decline in DRAM requirements is possible. (14)

A slowdown in AI investment would weaken HBM demand and could leave memory manufacturers with excess advanced DRAM capacity. (15)

Framework scorecard: All three are cyclical sellers. Micron's net cash of $68.3B and FCF of $32.9B in the latest quarter make it a survivor in any scenario. The risk is not survival — it is that current margins (87% gross) are unsustainable and the stock is priced for a cycle that may be closer to its peak than its midpoint. Seagate carries $1.4B net debt, which is manageable. Good businesses at potentially wrong prices if bought at peak-cycle multiples.

Note: SanDisk was spun off from Western Digital; no separate Bloomberg data was available for it as a standalone entity at the time of this analysis.

Thin-Margin Sellers — Dell, Super Micro
Current position: Revenue surging, margins thin, customer credit quality is the key variable.

Company            Latest Q Revenue Gross Margin Op Margin FCF (latest Q) Net Debt Working Capital
Dell (DELL)        $47.0B           21.1%        11.5%     $986M          $23.7B   -$3.2B         
Super Micro (SMCI) $19.9B           12.8%        10.4%     -$476M         $2.3B    +$22.4B        

Dell's AI-server revenue is expected to reach $19.3B in fiscal Q3 (results due 24 November), more than triple from a year ago, putting it on track to exceed its $74B full-year AI-server guidance. Bloomberg Intelligence sees room to raise the fiscal 2027 outlook, though uncertainties around neocloud funding, tighter AI-chip export controls, and the pace of US data-center permitting remain. (16)

Dell's negative working capital (-$3.2B) and $23.7B net debt are structural features of its business model, not acute distress signals. FCF has been positive in all four recent quarters, though compressed relative to revenue scale.

Super Micro's gross margin of 12.8% leaves almost no buffer. FCF swung from -$6.7B to +$719M across four quarters — a pattern consistent with lumpy working capital from large server orders. The $22.4B working capital position (largely inventory and receivables) is the key risk: if neocloud customers slow orders or face funding stress, SMCI's working capital unwinds rapidly.

Framework scorecard: Dell inherits neocloud customer credit risk. SMCI inherits it more acutely given thinner margins and more volatile FCF. Neither is a borrower-seat company, but both are exposed to borrower-seat customers. Customer funding quality is the deciding variable, per the framework.

Seller with Deal Overhang — Marvell
Current position: Solid fundamentals, but a $101B all-stock Lumentum acquisition creates the largest single overhang in the group.

