Revenue grew 70% over four quarters. Gross margin is stable at ~75% — exceptional for a hardware company. Net cash of $59.6B provides a substantial buffer. FCF is volatile quarter-to-quarter but strongly positive on a trailing basis.

The flag: inventory has nearly doubled from $21.4B to $39.7B over four quarters, while revenue grew ~70%. Inventory growing faster than revenue is the framework's stated warning sign for a seller. This is not yet at crisis levels, but it is the metric to watch at the February results.

NVIDIA had not provided a clear path for supply to catch up with demand over the following 12–18 months, with questions around large orders, Blackwell lead times, and supply-demand balance remaining unresolved. (17)

AI demand is strong, but sustaining 75% revenue growth is viewed as stretched against the pace of data-center construction, funding, and supply. (18)

Framework scorecard: Nvidia passes every survival filter criterion. Net cash, positive FCF, pricing power, scarce capacity. The lender-seat risk (vendor financing, purchase commitments) could not be fully quantified from standard Bloomberg fields — this requires direct review of Nvidia's 10-K footnotes. Likely long-term winner category, with inventory growth as the one active watch item.

