The decisive difference from 2000: The largest buyers (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta) are profitable and self-funded. In 2000, the buyers were also the borrowers. Today, the borrower-seat risk is concentrated in a smaller layer (CoreWeave, Nebius, neocloud operators) rather than the entire ecosystem. This makes a sector-wide simultaneous collapse less likely — but a borrower-seat wipeout remains a real scenario if the cycle pauses.

